diff --git a/AGENTS.md b/AGENTS.md
index de513a4..1b1c3c9 100644
--- a/AGENTS.md
+++ b/AGENTS.md
@@ -62,6 +62,18 @@ Parts use a `@N` numbering scheme:
 
 Each part gets a **unique color** (from `PART_PALETTE` or from `color()` in code). The label is rendered as a billboard overlay at the part's AABB center.
 
+A failed part keeps its number, so the other parts keep theirs. An exactly empty part also keeps its number and is reported as a warning.
+
+### Certainty badge
+
+Every label carries a small certainty badge (`PartLabel::badge`):
+
+- `✔` — the part succeeded. SynapsCAD evaluates everything under `GeometryContext::STRICT`, so a successful part's topology decisions are certified. There is no approximate state to show.
+- After the check mark, the qualifications recorded in the mesh's `construction_evidence()`: `approx-512` (`approximate_512_consumed`), `sampled` (`source_sampled`, e.g. a tessellated sphere), `projected` (`coordinates_projected`) and `no-history` (`source_history_missing`).
+- A failed part has no mesh, so its badge (`@N ✖ <kind> · line L:C`) is listed in the lower-left corner of the viewport, with the full message on hover. Failures are also posted to the chat panel.
+
+The AI context (`build_part_context` in `ai_chat.rs`) lists every part in number order with the same information: color and evidence for successful parts, failure kind, source location and message for failed parts.
+
 ### Why This Matters for Agents
 
 When a user says _"make @2 taller"_ or _"change the color of @1"_, the AI agent knows exactly which geometric part is being referenced. The label system provides:
@@ -157,6 +169,13 @@ if ($view == "assembly") view_assembly();
 
 When adding new features, use `self.warnings.push(...)` in the `Evaluator` for recoverable issues, and `return Err(...)` for fatal per-part failures.
 
+**Keep the exact stack's typed failures and certainty.** csgrs and the Hyper crates return typed failures and a certainty receipt (`Certified` or `Approximate512Consumed`) with every result.
+
+- A failed shape is `Shape::Failed(ShapeFailure)` (`src/compiler/failure.rs`). It keeps a `FailureKind` (csgrs's `ValidationErrorKind` via `Error::kind()`, or a SynapsCAD kind such as `InvalidArgument`, `MixedDimensions`, `Undecided`, `Unsupported` or `RenderConversion`), the underlying `csgrs::Error` as its `source()`, a human message, and the openscad-rs `Span` of the innermost statement that produced it (attached in `Evaluator::eval_statement`).
+- Wrap a csgrs error with `ShapeFailure::kernel(context, error)`; never format it into a `String` before display.
+- `compile_scad_code` returns failed top-level parts as `failed_parts: Vec<FailedPart>`, with their part number and source line and column. Successful parts carry `PartEvidence`, read from the mesh's `construction_evidence()`.
+- Do not drop a result's certainty when the value is unwrapped; carry it with the part so it can be shown.
+
 ### Running
 
 ```sh
@@ -247,6 +266,22 @@ Before every `git push`, agents must:
 
 A push is not allowed while any required check is failing. Hosted deployment actions, secret-dependent publication, and packaging for operating systems unavailable on the current host may be excluded only when they cannot be reproduced locally; inspect those workflow steps for consistency and report the limitation explicitly.
 
+## Planned work
+
+These directions come from an external review (3 November 2026). They are tracked with the stack's simplification and API program in csgrs (`docs/research/simplification-and-api-program.md`, packages D01-D03). Items 1-3 are implemented (see *Error Handling*, *Certainty badge* and the note below); item 4 is open.
+
+1. **Typed failures with source spans.**
+   - Replace `Shape::Failed(String)` with a typed failure that keeps the underlying csgrs/hypermesh error and the openscad-rs source span.
+   - Render the message only at display.
+   - The review cites alumina-interface as an example of carrying evidence through to the UI.
+2. **A certainty badge per part.** Show, in the viewport and in the part labels, whether each part was certified or used approximate decisions, and why a part failed. The exactness of the result is the selling point, so it must be visible.
+3. **One Boolean program per subtree.**
+   - `eval_boolean_op` folds children pairwise, so `union()` over N children builds N−1 separate arrangements.
+   - Lower each `union`/`difference`/`intersection` subtree into one hypermesh `BooleanProgram`, so intersection work is shared. Use csgrs's lazy CSG tree once it exists.
+   - Measure the change with `benches/compile_default.rs` on the same completed output.
+   - Implemented with `csgrs::SolidExpr`: a 3D Boolean is recorded as `Shape::Csg3D` (balanced for long child lists), nested Boolean subtrees and transforms are recorded into the same expression, and `Shape::resolve` evaluates it once under `GeometryContext::STRICT`. Top-level parts, `hull()`, `minkowski()`, `center` and extrusion children resolve it. 2D regions keep their pairwise region Booleans.
+4. **Differential corpus.** Evaluate openscad-rs's OpenSCAD test-suite corpus in SynapsCAD and diff the results against OpenSCAD with its Manifold backend. Classify every difference rather than silently absorbing it.
+
 ## Maintaining This Document
 
 **Keep `AGENTS.md` up to date.** When making architecture-relevant changes — new plugins, new resources/components, changed data flow, new UI patterns, or new conventions — update this file so that AI agents always have accurate context about the codebase.
